Deploy Kerbside with Kolla-Ansible.

This change implements the Kerbside Kolla-Ansible deployment role. It is a
fairly large change, but I couldn't find a meaningful way to break it up
into smaller chunks.

The basic idea is that there is a proxy sitting in front of the Nova
hypervisors that can talk the SPICE protocol to clients, lookup where the
console is on the relevant hypervisor, and then build a SPICE connection
to that hypervisor. It then shovels SPICE packets back and forth between
the two.

That proxy is called Kerbside and it consists of two components which
each have their own Kolla container. kerbside-api handles business logic
and is a uwsgi app. kerbside-proxy is the SPICE native component. Because
kerbside-proxy is not talking HTTP to clients, and expects TLS encrypted
traffic on the secure port, it uses the frontend certificates to do TLS
itself, and haproxy simply acts as a layer four load balancer.

Because the proxy terminates TLS for every console it serves, whatever
kerbside_enable_tls_backend says about the REST API, it needs the backend
certificate and key wherever it runs. service-cert-copy places those only
on hosts in the tls-backend group, which is control and network, so the
role copies them itself for the Kerbside hosts and uses service-cert-copy
for the CA bundle alone. Without that, pointing the vdiproxy group at
dedicated hosts -- the reason it is a separate group -- leaves the proxy
with no certificate and it fails to start.

The SPICE ports are the only tcp services in the tree exposed externally,
and haproxy's single external frontend was written for http: its SNI map
is built from http services alone, and the frontend emitter only skipped
the external http and redirect cases. An external tcp frontend was
therefore emitted next to its internal twin, binding the same address and
port wherever the internal and external VIPs are the same, and haproxy
refused the configuration. It is now skipped on the grounds the redirect
case already was. The proxy's external_fqdn cannot route the SPICE ports
in that mode either, which the defaults now say.

